id:                     E432
trigger_tool: clang|gcc|native-sdk|code-review
phase: source-adaptation|runtime-safety
error_signature: native API failure returns non-null owned output or external coordinate overflows signed target type
severity: 2
category: native-api-contract
match_keywords: native SDK, output ownership, failure status, destroy, finite coordinate, int32 overflow, checked narrowing
title: Release native error outputs and range-check external coordinates before narrowing
root_cause: Native API status, output ownership, and numeric representability are independent contracts. A nonzero status does not prove an output pointer is null, while floating-point offsets and signed deltas can exceed the consumer coordinate range before or during arithmetic.
affected_scope: C and C++ platform adapters that consume owned native output handles or combine external floating-point positions, signed origins, and relative deltas.
verification_cmd: Compile the production helper and run finite, NaN, infinity, half-unit rounding, INT32_MIN, INT32_MAX, and both overflow directions; assert every non-null failure output reaches its exact destructor before return.
rec_priority: 1
rec_approach: ownership-and-checked-narrowing
rec_description: Release every non-null owned output on all status paths. Reject non-finite values, check rounded offsets against origin-adjusted int32 bounds, perform addition in int64, and narrow only after the final range check.
rec_command: add scoped or explicit native cleanup; add checked coordinate helpers and boundary tests
rec_auto_fix: false
rec_impl: manual
fallback_priority: 2
fallback_approach: fail-closed-value-rejection
fallback_description: If the exact valid layout cannot be established, reject unrepresentable values and retain the last validated coordinate instead of clamping or overflowing.
fallback_command: log the invalid external tuple and return without native injection
fallback_auto_fix: false
fallback_impl: manual
forbid_1_pattern: status\s*!=\s*0.*return.*without.*destroy|origin\s*\+\s*static_cast<int32
forbid_1_reason: Failure can still transfer an owned object, and narrowing before checked addition can overflow or invoke an out-of-range conversion.
forbid_1_consequence: Resource leaks, undefined behavior, or incorrect pointer injection.
forbid_2_pattern: clamp.*INT32|ignore.*NaN.*test
forbid_2_reason: Silent clamping changes pointer semantics, while missing non-finite tests leaves an external-value hole.
forbid_2_consequence: Boundary failures become platform-specific cursor jumps instead of explicit rejection.
causes: []
caused_by: []
similar_to: []
conflicts_with: []
prerequisite_of: []
